course: dictionary
lesson: 1991
title: A strong force
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A compulsion is a very strong pull to do something, often something hard to stop, even when you know you shouldn't. It can come from inside you, like a wish you can't shake off, or from outside you, when someone forces you to act.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence: «Under severe compulsion from the Badger» the barge-woman was found and paid the value of her horse. The words "severe" and "under" show that someone was pushing hard. Toad «kicked terribly at this», so he did not want to do it, but he had to.
explain: Try the swap check. Put "forced" in place of "compulsion" and read it again: "Under severe forced from the Badger" is clumsy, but "Under severe pressure from the Badger" makes sense, so compulsion means a strong force pushing a person to act.
fact: A compulsion is a very strong wish to do something that is hard to stop.
fact: "Under compulsion" means being forced to do something by a rule or by another person.
fact: «Under severe compulsion from the Badger» shows Toad was made to pay, even though he did not want to.
fact: Compulsion can be a feeling inside someone, such as a compulsion to check things again and again.
fact: "No compulsion" means nobody is forcing you, so you are free to choose.
exercise: In «Under severe compulsion from the Badger», what does compulsion mean? | A gentle wish to be kind | A prize for good work | A long walk home | *Being forced to act by another person
exercise: Maya says, "I have a compulsion to check the door is locked, again and again." What does this suggest? | *A strong urge that is hard to stop | A prize she won | A rule her teacher set | A calm, relaxed feeling
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Toad kicked terribly at this» that shows he did not want to do it. | *kicked | this | Toad | at
test: The sign says "No compulsion to attend." What does this mean? | You are very keen to go | You can only go on Sundays | *You are not forced to go | You must go every day
test: "Under compulsion" means | Lying down to rest | Happily chosen by you | *Forced to do something by a rule or person | Playing a fast game
exam: Which sentence uses compulsion to mean a strong inner urge that is hard to resist? | The rule was a compulsion for everyone to wear a helmet | Mia won a compulsion at the fair | *Ben felt a compulsion to bite his nails, even when he tried to stop | The compulsion was printed on the sign at the gate
